<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Province of Georgia</h1><p class="st-meta">1732 CE – 1776 CE · capital Savannah</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last of Britain's thirteen mainland colonies, founded by James Oglethorpe as a buffer against Spanish Florida and an experiment without slavery.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>In 1732 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-ii-of-great-britain">George II</a> granted a charter to a board of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trustees-for-the-establishment-of-the-colony-of-georgia-in-america">Trustees</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">James Oglethorpe</a> for the land between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altamaha-river">Altamaha</a> rivers. Oglethorpe founded Savannah in 1733, with the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yamacraw">Yamacraw</a> leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomochichi">Tomochichi</a>. The colony was meant to shield the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-south-carolina">Province of South Carolina</a> from <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida</a> and to offer a new start to the poor.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trustees-for-the-establishment-of-the-colony-of-georgia-in-america">Trustees</a> banned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, limited landholdings and forbade hard liquor, and they recruited Protestant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia-salzburgers">Salzburgers</a>, Scottish Highlanders and others. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-oglethorpe">Oglethorpe</a> defeated a Spanish invasion at Bloody Marsh in 1742. Settlers, envious of Carolina's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> wealth, pressed for slavery, which was allowed in 1751. The Trustees surrendered the charter, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> became a royal province in 1752, with rice plantations spreading along the coast under governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-wright-governor">James Wright</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Patriots took control in 1776 and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/georgia">Georgia</a> became a state of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>, though British forces reoccupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/savannah">Savannah</a> from 1778 to 1782 and restored royal government there. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a> adopted its first state constitution in 1777.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/province-of-georgia.jpg" alt="Province of Georgia" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: AnonMoos, based on image by Zscout370, AnonMoos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Red_ensign_of_Great_Britain_(1707%E2%80%931800,_square_canton).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1732 CE – 1776 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Savannah</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>James Oglethorpe, John Reynolds, James Wright</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>United States</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Georgia (colony)</dd></dl></aside></div>
